<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Histria</h1><p class="st-meta">657 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.) · capital Histria</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The oldest Greek colony on the western Black Sea coast, founded by Miletus near the mouth of the Danube.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Milesian colonists founded Histria c. 657 BCE on a peninsula by the sea near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> delta, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a>. It was named after the Istros, the Greek name for the Danube, and became the earliest Greek city on the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Histria traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, wine and pottery with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/getae">Getae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> for grain, fish, honey and slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> mentions its shift from oligarchy to democracy. Its sanctuaries honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus-polieus">Zeus Polieus</a> and Aphrodite, and it struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins showing two heads and a sea eagle catching a dolphin.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Histria was garrisoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> and revolted against him in 313 BCE. Its harbour silted up. In 72 to 71 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-terentius-varro-lucullus">Marcus Lucullus</a> brought the western Pontic cities under Roman control, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burebista">Burebista's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/getae">Getae</a> sacked the city soon after. It revived under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and was abandoned in the 7th century CE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 657 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:46.63%;width:11.64%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>657 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Histria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Apollo</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Istros, Istria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-364&amp;focus=histria" aria-label="Histria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5367 602 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Histria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5687 858L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5687 858h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5687 858h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Histria. <a href="/atlas?year=-364&amp;focus=histria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

